// Title: Linux DNS Tunneling C2 via Dnscat
// Sigma ID: f6c1d4b8-8a5e-4d07-f9b6-7e2c5a1d4f80
// Status: experimental | Level: high
// Author: Ivan Saakov
// Date: 2026-05-10 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: command-and-control, T1071.004
// Description: Detects execution of dnscat or dnscat2, a post-exploitation tool that establishes an encrypted
//   command-and-control channel using DNS queries and responses. DNS traffic is permitted in most
//   network environments and is rarely inspected, making dnscat an effective covert channel for C2
//   communication, data exfiltration, and remote command execution that bypasses traditional network
//   security controls.
// False positives:
//   - Authorized red team exercise using dnscat for adversary simulation
//   - Security research in an isolated lab environment
// References:
//   - https://github.com/iagox86/dnscat2
//   - https://www.sans.org/blog/dns-data-exfiltration-what-is-it-and-how-do-you-stop-it/
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/e5bac28da3655c33390db7ef1503db8cd3babb5f/rules/linux/process_creation/proc_creation_lnx_dnscat_execution.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where (FolderPath endswith "/dnscat" or FolderPath endswith "/dnscat2") or (ProcessCommandLine contains "dnscat" or ProcessCommandLine contains "dnscat2")
